course: dictionary
lesson: 12352
title: Who can we believe?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Credibility is how far people believe and trust someone or something. A source with credibility is one you can rely on. When you read or watch something, ask yourself: is this believable?
explain: Three questions help you judge credibility. Who wrote it? Can the facts be checked? Do other good sources agree? «The news report named three experts and checked its facts, which gave it credibility.» The writer showed their work, so readers could trust it.
explain: Credibility can be lost, and it can be hard to win back. «After she was caught lying twice, Mia lost her credibility with the class.» Her friends did not stop being kind, but they no longer believed what she said.
fact: Credibility means the quality of being believed and trusted.
fact: Credibility is an uncountable noun, so you say «some credibility» or «more credibility», not «a credibility».
fact: You can lose credibility by lying, and it can take a long time to win back.
fact: Useful phrases are «lose credibility», «damage your credibility» and «a credibility problem».
fact: In court, a witness's credibility matters, because the judge and jury decide whether to believe what they say.
exercise: «The website had no author and no sources, so it had little credibility.» What does credibility mean here? | *Being believed and trusted | Being very long | Being very cheap | Being popular with lots of people
exercise: Which thing helps a source have more credibility? | *It names real experts and its facts can be checked | It is written in capital letters | It uses lots of bright colours | It has no author
exercise: «After she was caught lying twice, Mia lost her credibility with the class.» Copy the words that show Mia was trusted less. | caught lying twice | *lost her credibility | Mia | with the class
test: Why does a witness's credibility matter in court? | Witnesses must always wear a uniform | It shows how tall the witness is | It makes the courtroom quieter | *The judge and jury must decide whether to believe them
test: Which of these is a good check of a news story's credibility? | *Can its facts be checked, and do other good sources agree? | Is it written in bold letters? | Does it make you laugh? | Does it have a big photo on the front?
exam: Which sentence is the best example of damaging your credibility? | *A politician was caught making up numbers, and after that nobody trusted what he said | A politician wore a blue tie to the meeting | A politician arrived early to the meeting | A politician gave a speech about his favourite food
